0.10235856,0.00995818,0.02364903,-0.02012914,0.01102963,0.03488778,-0.09545126,0.03652434,0.09239685,-0.0423791,0.02610465,0.09401561,-0.00659227,-0.03702249,0.01607435,-0.04822833,-0.00622953,0.01589344,-0.05672551,0.01508086,0.0112262,-0.01804804,-0.04083997,-0.03557069,-0.0223413,-0.00411964,-0.07995832,-0.01522902,-0.05925707,0.03274785,0.01178474,-0.11541656,-0.04642312,-0.003586,-0.04872021,0.04381109,-0.08150706,-0.01251319,-0.02940091,-0.00086034,0.00154951,-0.03073625,0.02689046,0.04696368,-0.04121459,0.06137379,-0.00871227,0.02728902,-0.01577383,0.02017938,-0.0332562,-0.00859859,-0.01553592,0.01663636,0.09830202,0.00996288,-0.01200358,0.00549811,0.04113327,-0.03307574,-0.17880212,-0.00590515,0.02044585,-0.07931962,0.07111891,0.00407312,0.01363492,-0.03026032,0.07599217,0.05219571,0.0284128,-0.02318817,0.00600511,-0.02799219,0.0370449,-0.0310148,-0.04986114,0.00804998,-0.03206678,0.03599824,-0.02369641,0.02622855,-0.07434524,-0.10305311,0.03184704,-0.03857592,0.11289153,0.01962497,0.05683188,0.01397525,0.00358241,-0.01256321,-0.02236397,-0.06826247,0.06903332,0.0354041,-0.02929343,-0.00752557,-0.06449948,-0.08956462,0.02346267,0.06150636,-0.00631867,-0.0324458,-0.10433605,-0.02255009,0.04930287,0.02083519,-0.02563245,0.01115236,-0.00877879,-0.02493684,0.03550611,-0.0294085,0.02164758,-0.06141926,-0.06920132,-0.00391896,0.01241733,0.01470698,0.0095247,0.07239377,0.00136876,-0.00078789,0.00623366,-0.05712011,-0.0025832,-0.0624484,0.03423882,-0.02917474,-0.01139368,0.09897137,-0.00527287,-0.00253085,0.07337242,-0.00084349,0.00931475,-0.01005224,-0.01177767,-0.02381156,0.04614789,-0.09622364,0.06618728,0.06233418,0.05933573,-0.00227443,0.05260321,0.04683875,0.03213874,0.03654306,0.0133105,-0.02207691,-0.02300424,-0.009699,0.0438462,-0.03684333,-0.25116858,0.03351844,0.0065811,0.03400506,0.02658783,-0.05896936,0.06676749,-0.00578597,-0.09360868,0.01615664,0.02194152,-0.00336803,0.05604385,-0.04108935,0.01439859,-0.02017008,0.05537401,-0.01045586,0.03384376,-0.01526428,0.00605005,0.03549574,0.1953416,-0.02364462,0.11627086,0.05058016,0.01408281,0.04208802,0.07983591,0.05074027,0.06646632,-0.02012029,0.07099114,-0.08786973,0.02214205,-0.00735305,-0.02886299,0.05176919,0.0274571,-0.01695126,-0.00992979,0.00348818,-0.01767342,0.00207049,0.16388856,0.01856987,0.0464031,-0.10444437,-0.0328918,0.06325289,0.00393634,-0.02351998,0.01697874,-0.01235088,-0.02709364,0.0781515,-0.01972871,-0.01357129,0.01198285,-0.04802784,0.02221774,-0.0644756,0.06166761,0.01028202,0.00762457],"last_embed":{"hash":"1hcqcip","tokens":454}}},"last_read":{"hash":"1hcqcip","at":1778862431732},"class_name":"SmartSource","last_import":{"mtime":1778862423378,"size":18457,"at":1778862431661,"hash":"1hcqcip"},"blocks":{"#---frontmatter---":[1,4],"#Unit 5: Markov Chains - 5 Questions with Full Solutions --- ## 🧠 Before We Start: The Golden Rule of Markov Chains > **\"The future depends ONLY on the present, NOT on the past\"** Think of it like this: If you want to predict tomorrow's weather, you only need to know **today's weather**. It doesn't matter what the weather was last week. --- ## 📖 Basic Concepts You Need to Know ### What is a State? A **state** is simply the **current situation** of a system. - Example: Weather can be in state \"Sunny\" or \"Rainy\" - Example: A student can be in state \"Studying\" or \"Not Studying\" ### What is a Transition Matrix? It's a table that shows **\"If I'm here now, what's the probability I go there next?\"** ``` Example: Weather System Tomorrow Sunny Rainy Today Sunny [ 0.7 0.3 ] Rainy [ 0.4 0.6 ] Reading: If it's Sunny today → 70% chance Sunny tomorrow → 30% chance Rainy tomorrow ``` ### Key Rules: ``` ✅ Every row must ADD UP TO 1.0 ✅ All probabilities must be BETWEEN 0 and 1 ✅ P(n) means the matrix after n steps = P multiplied n times ``` --- --- # ❓ Question 1 (Easy - Basic Transition Probability) ## The Problem: > A college student named Arjun has the following study habits: > - If he **studies** one night, there is a **70% chance he studies** the next night and **30% chance he doesn't** > - If he **doesn't study** one night, there is **60% chance he studies** the next night and **40% chance he doesn't** > > **On Monday night, Arjun studies.** > > Find: > **(i) The Transition Probability Matrix** > **(ii) Probability that Arjun studies on Wednesday night** > **(iii) Probability that Arjun doesn't study on Wednesday night** --- ## ✅ Full Solution: ### 🔵 Step 1: Understand the States We have **2 states**: - **State 1** = Studies (S) - **State 2** = Doesn't Study (D) ``` Think of Arjun switching between two modes every night: Monday → Tuesday → Wednesday → ... S ? ? ``` --- ### 🔵 Step 2: Write the Transition Probability Matrix From the problem: ``` FROM State S (Studies): → Goes to S with probability 0.7 → Goes to D with probability 0.3 FROM State D (Doesn't Study): → Goes to S with probability 0.6 → Goes to D with probability 0.4 ``` $$\\boxed{P = \\begin{bmatrix} 0.7 & 0.3 \\\\ 0.6 & 0.4 \\end{bmatrix}}$$ ``` To S To D From S [ 0.7 0.3 ] From D [ 0.6 0.4 ] ``` **Check: Each row adds to 1:** - Row 1: 0.7 + 0.3 = 1.0 ✅ - Row 2: 0.6 + 0.4 = 1.0 ✅ --- ### 🔵 Step 3: Set Up the Initial State Arjun **studies on Monday** = He starts in **State S** We write this as a **probability vector** (a row showing where we start): $$\\text{Initial vector} = \\begin{bmatrix} 1 & 0 \\end{bmatrix}$$ ``` [1, 0] means: 100% chance he's in State S (Studies) on Monday 0% chance he's in State D ``` --- ### 🔵 Step 4: Find Tuesday's Probabilities (1 step) Multiply the initial vector by P: $$\\text{Tuesday} = \\begin{bmatrix} 1 & 0 \\end{bmatrix} \\times \\begin{bmatrix} 0.7 & 0.3 \\\\ 0.6 & 0.4 \\end{bmatrix}$$ **How to multiply:** ``` Tuesday S probability = (1 × 0.7) + (0 × 0.6) = 0.7 Tuesday D probability = (1 × 0.3) + (0 × 0.4) = 0.3 ``` $$\\text{Tuesday} = \\begin{bmatrix} 0.7 & 0.3 \\end{bmatrix}$$ ``` So on Tuesday: 70% chance Arjun studies 30% chance Arjun doesn't study ``` --- ### 🔵 Step 5: Find Wednesday's Probabilities (2 steps) Multiply Tuesday's vector by P again: $$\\text{Wednesday} = \\begin{bmatrix} 0.7 & 0.3 \\end{bmatrix} \\times \\begin{bmatrix} 0.7 & 0.3 \\\\ 0.6 & 0.4 \\end{bmatrix}$$ **How to multiply:** ``` Wednesday S = (0.7 × 0.7) + (0.3 × 0.6) = 0.49 + 0.18 = 0.67 Wednesday D = (0.7 × 0.3) + (0.3 × 0.4) = 0.21 + 0.12 = 0.33 ``` $$\\text{Wednesday} = \\begin{bmatrix} 0.67 & 0.33 \\end{bmatrix}$$ --- ### 🔵 Final Answers: > **(i)** Transition Matrix: $P = \\begin{bmatrix} 0.7 & 0.3 \\\\ 0.6 & 0.4 \\end{bmatrix}$ > **(ii) Probability Arjun studies on Wednesday = 0.67 (67%)** > **(iii) Probability Arjun doesn't study on Wednesday = 0.33 (33%)** ``` INTUITION CHECK: He starts by studying. Study habit is fairly strong (70%). So it makes sense that 2 days later, there's still a 67% chance he studies. Makes sense! ✅ ``` --- --- # ❓ Question 2 (Medium - Finding Specific Path Probability) ## The Problem: > A Markov chain has **3 states: 1, 2, 3** with the following transition probability matrix: > > $$P = \\begin{bmatrix} 0.1 & 0.5 & 0.4 \\\\ 0.6 & 0.2 & 0.2 \\\\ 0.3 & 0.4 & 0.3 \\end{bmatrix}$$ > > The initial distribution is $P^{(0)} = (0.7, 0.2, 0.1)$ > > Find: > **(i)** $P(X_2 = 3, X_1 = 3, X_0 = 2)$ > **(ii)** $P(X_3 = 2, X_2 = 3, X_1 = 3, X_0 = 2)$ --- ## ✅ Full Solution: ### 🔵 Step 1: Understand What the Question is Asking The notation $X_0, X_1, X_2, X_3$ means the **state at time 0, 1, 2, 3**. ``` Part (i): P(X₂=3, X₁=3, X₀=2) In plain English: \"What is the probability that: - At time 0, the system is in State 2 AND - At time 1, the system is in State 3 AND - At time 2, the system is in State 3\" It's like asking: What's the probability of the PATH: 2 → 3 → 3? ``` --- ### 🔵 Step 2: The Formula for Path Probability $$P(X_2=3, X_1=3, X_0=2) = P(X_0=2) \\times P_{23} \\times P_{33}$$ Where: - $P(X_0 = 2)$ = probability of **starting** in state 2 - $P_{23}$ = probability of going **from state 2 to state 3** - $P_{33}$ = probability of **staying in state 3** ``` Think of it like: Probability of PATH = (Start here) × (Go here) × (Go here) PATH: 2 → 3 → 3 = P(start at 2) × P(2→3) × P(3→3) ``` --- ### 🔵 Step 3: Read Values from the Matrix **Finding P(X₀ = 2):** From initial distribution $(0.7, 0.2, 0.1)$: ``` P(X₀ = 1) = 0.7 P(X₀ = 2) = 0.2 ← This is what we need P(X₀ = 3) = 0.1 ``` **Finding P₂₃ (going from State 2 to State 3):** Look at **Row 2, Column 3** of matrix P: $$P = \\begin{bmatrix} 0.1 & 0.5 & 0.4 \\\\ 0.6 & 0.2 & \\mathbf{0.2} \\\\ 0.3 & 0.4 & 0.3 \\end{bmatrix}$$ ``` Row 2 = [0.6, 0.2, 0.2] ↑ ↑ ↑ P(2→1) P(2→2) P(2→3) So P₂₃ = 0.2 ``` **Finding P₃₃ (staying in State 3):** Look at **Row 3, Column 3**: $$P_{33} = 0.3$$ --- ### 🔵 Step 4: Calculate Part (i) $$P(X_2=3, X_1=3, X_0=2) = P(X_0=2) \\times P_{23} \\times P_{33}$$ $$= 0.2 \\times 0.2 \\times 0.3$$ $$= \\boxed{0.012}$$ ``` Meaning: There's a 1.2% chance of following the path: Start at State 2 → Go to State 3 → Stay at State 3 ``` --- ### 🔵 Step 5: Calculate Part (ii) $$P(X_3=2, X_2=3, X_1=3, X_0=2) = P(X_0=2) \\times P_{23} \\times P_{33} \\times P_{32}$$ **New value needed - P₃₂ (going from State 3 to State 2):** Look at **Row 3, Column 2**: ``` Row 3 = [0.3, 0.4, 0.3] ↑ ↑ ↑ P(3→1) P(3→2) P(3→3) So P₃₂ = 0.4 ``` $$= 0.2 \\times 0.2 \\times 0.3 \\times 0.4$$ $$= 0.012 \\times 0.4$$ $$= \\boxed{0.0048}$$ ``` Meaning: There's a 0.48% chance of following the path: State 2 → State 3 → State 3 → State 2 ``` --- --- # ❓ Question 3 (Medium - Finding Limiting/Steady State Probabilities) ## The Problem: > A weather system can be in two states: > - **State 1:** Sunny > - **State 2:** Rainy > > The transition matrix is: > $$P = \\begin{bmatrix} 0.8 & 0.2 \\\\ 0.5 & 0.5 \\end{bmatrix}$$ > > **Find the long-run probabilities** (i.e., in the long run, what fraction of days will be Sunny and what fraction will be Rainy?) --- ## ✅ Full Solution: ### 🔵 Step 1: Understand What \"Long Run\" Means ``` Imagine the weather system running for 1000 days. After a very long time, the system \"settles\" into a pattern. No matter where you start, you'll find: - Some fixed % of days are Sunny - Some fixed % of days are Rainy These fixed percentages are called LIMITING PROBABILITIES or STEADY STATE PROBABILITIES. We call them: π₁ (for Sunny) and π₂ (for Rainy) ``` --- ### 🔵 Step 2: Set Up the Equations The **magic equation** for limiting probabilities is: $$\\pi P = \\pi$$ In plain English: \"The distribution doesn't change anymore\" Let $\\pi = [\\pi_1, \\pi_2]$ Expanding $\\pi P = \\pi$: $$[\\pi_1, \\pi_2] \\begin{bmatrix} 0.8 & 0.2 \\\\ 0.5 & 0.5 \\end{bmatrix} = [\\pi_1, \\pi_2]$$ --- ### 🔵 Step 3: Multiply the Left Side **For column 1 (Sunny):** ``` (π₁ × 0.8) + (π₂ × 0.5) = π₁ ``` **For column 2 (Rainy):** ``` (π₁ × 0.2) + (π₂ × 0.5) = π₂ ``` --- ### 🔵 Step 4: Solve the Equations **From Equation 1:** $$0.8\\pi_1 + 0.5\\pi_2 = \\pi_1$$ $$0.5\\pi_2 = \\pi_1 - 0.8\\pi_1$$ $$0.5\\pi_2 = 0.2\\pi_1$$ $$\\pi_2 = \\frac{0.2}{0.5}\\pi_1 = 0.4\\pi_1 \\quad \\cdots (A)$$ **We also know probabilities must add to 1:** $$\\pi_1 + \\pi_2 = 1 \\quad \\cdots (B)$$ **Substitute (A) into (B):** $$\\pi_1 + 0.4\\pi_1 = 1$$ $$1.4\\pi_1 = 1$$ $$\\pi_1 = \\frac{1}{1.4} = \\frac{5}{7} \\approx \\boxed{0.714}$$ $$\\pi_2 = 1 - 0.714 = \\boxed{0.286}$$ --- ### 🔵 Step 5: Verify Our Answer Check that $\\pi P = \\pi$: $$[0.714, 0.286] \\begin{bmatrix} 0.8 & 0.2 \\\\ 0.5 & 0.5 \\end{bmatrix}$$ ``` Column 1: (0.714 × 0.8) + (0.286 × 0.5) = 0.5712 + 0.143 = 0.7142 ≈ 0.714 ✅ Column 2: (0.714 × 0.2) + (0.286 × 0.5) = 0.1428 + 0.143 = 0.2858 ≈ 0.286 ✅ ``` --- ### 🔵 Final Answer: > **In the long run:** > - **71.4% of days will be Sunny** > - **28.6% of days will be Rainy** ``` INTUITION CHECK: Sunny days have a STRONG tendency to stay sunny (80%). Rainy days have a weaker tendency to stay rainy (50%). So it makes sense that sunny days dominate in the long run. 71.4% sunny seems very reasonable! ✅ ``` --- --- # ❓ Question 4 (Medium-Hard - 3 State Limiting Probability) ## The Problem: > Three boys A, B, C are throwing a ball to each other. > - **A always throws to B** > - **B always throws to C** > - **C is equally likely to throw to A or B** (50-50 chance) > > Find: > **(i) The Transition Probability Matrix** > **(ii) The limiting probabilities (long run fraction of time each person holds the ball)** --- ## ✅ Full Solution: ### 🔵 Step 1: Understand the System ``` States: A has ball, B has ball, C has ball Let's track where the ball goes: If A has ball → Always throws to B State A → State B (probability = 1) If B has ball → Always throws to C State B → State C (probability = 1) If C has ball → Throws to A or B equally State C → State A (probability = 0.5) State C → State B (probability = 0.5) ``` --- ### 🔵 Step 2: Build the Transition Matrix ``` Ball goes TO: A B C Ball A [ 0 1 0 ] ← A always gives to B from B [ 0 0 1 ] ← B always gives to C C [ 0.5 0.5 0 ] ← C gives to A or B equally ``` $$\\boxed{P = \\begin{bmatrix} 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0.5 & 0.5 & 0 \\end{bmatrix}}$$ **Check rows add to 1:** - Row A: 0 + 1 + 0 = 1 ✅ - Row B: 0 + 0 + 1 = 1 ✅ - Row C: 0.5 + 0.5 + 0 = 1 ✅ --- ### 🔵 Step 3: Set Up Limiting Probability Equations Let $\\pi = [\\pi_A, \\pi_B, \\pi_C]$ Using $\\pi P = \\pi$: $$[\\pi_A, \\pi_B, \\pi_C] \\begin{bmatrix} 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0.5 & 0.5 & 0 \\end{bmatrix} = [\\pi_A, \\pi_B, \\pi_C]$$ --- ### 🔵 Step 4: Write Out the 3 Equations **For πₐ (probability of being at A):** ``` (πₐ × 0) + (π_B × 0) + (π_C × 0.5) = πₐ 0.5π_C = πₐ ... (1) ``` **For π_B (probability of being at B):** ``` (πₐ × 1) + (π_B × 0) + (π_C × 0.5) = π_B πₐ + 0.5π_C = π_B ... (2) ``` **For π_C (probability of being at C):** ``` (πₐ × 0) + (π_B × 1) + (π_C × 0) = π_C π_B = π_C ... (3) ``` **Plus the condition that all probabilities sum to 1:** $$\\pi_A + \\pi_B + \\pi_C = 1 \\quad \\cdots (4)$$ --- ### 🔵 Step 5: Solve the System **From equation (3):** $$\\pi_B = \\pi_C$$ **From equation (1):** $$\\pi_A = 0.5\\pi_C$$ **Substitute into equation (4):** $$0.5\\pi_C + \\pi_C + \\pi_C = 1$$ $$2.5\\pi_C = 1$$ $$\\pi_C = \\frac{1}{2.5} = 0.4$$ **Therefore:** $$\\pi_B = \\pi_C = 0.4$$ $$\\pi_A = 0.5 \\times 0.4 = 0.2$$ --- ### 🔵 Verify with Equation (2): $$\\pi_A + 0.5\\pi_C = \\pi_B$$ $$0.2 + 0.5(0.4) = 0.2 + 0.2 = 0.4 = \\pi_B \\checkmark$$ --- ### 🔵 Final Answer: > **In the long run:** > - **A holds the ball 20% of the time** ($\\pi_A = 0.2$) > - **B holds the ball 40% of the time** ($\\pi_B = 0.4$) > - **C holds the ball 40% of the time** ($\\pi_C = 0.4$) ``` INTUITION CHECK: - A only RECEIVES the ball from C (50% of C's throws) - B RECEIVES from both A (100%) and C (50%) - C RECEIVES from B (100%) B and C both receive from multiple sources, so they hold the ball more often than A. Makes total sense! ✅ ``` --- --- # ❓ Question 5 (Hard - Classification of States) ## The Problem: > A Markov chain has the following transition probability matrix: > > $$P = \\begin{bmatrix} 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1/2 & 0 & 1/2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\end{bmatrix}$$ > > **Classify all the states of this Markov chain.** > (Are they Transient or Recurrent? Periodic or Aperiodic? Ergodic?) --- ## ✅ Full Solution: ### 🔵 Step 1: Understand the Classification Terms ``` TRANSIENT STATE: → The system MIGHT never come back here → Like a bus stop where buses might skip you RECURRENT STATE: → The system WILL definitely come back here eventually → Like your home - you always return PERIODIC STATE (period d): → You can only return to this state every d steps → Example: Period 2 means you return after 2, 4, 6... steps only APERIODIC STATE: → Period = 1 → You can return at any time (2 steps, 3 steps, etc.) ERGODIC STATE: → Recurrent + Aperiodic → Best kind of state for analysis ``` --- ### 🔵 Step 2: Draw the Transition Diagram First, let's see which states can reach which: $$P = \\begin{bmatrix} 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1/2 & 0 & 1/2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\end{bmatrix}$$ ``` Reading the matrix: State 1: Goes to State 2 (prob=1), nowhere else State 2: Goes to State 1 (prob=1/2) or State 3 (prob=1/2) State 3: Goes to State 2 (prob=1), nowhere else Drawing the diagram: (1/2) (1/2) 1 ←─────── 2 ──────────→ 3 │ ↑ │ └──────────→│ │ (1) │←────────────┘ (1) (1) ``` More clearly: ``` 1 ──(prob 1)──→ 2 ──(prob 1/2)──→ 3 ↑ │ │ │ │ (prob 1/2) │ (prob 1) └─────────────────────┘ │ ↑ │ └────────────────────────┘ ``` --- ### 🔵 Step 3: Check if States Communicate Two states **communicate** if you can get from one to the other AND back. **Can State 1 reach State 2?** - State 1 → State 2 directly (prob = 1) ✅ YES **Can State 2 reach State 1?** - State 2 → State 1 directly (prob = 1/2) ✅ YES **So State 1 and State 2 communicate** ↔ **Can State 2 reach State 3?** - State 2 → State 3 directly (prob = 1/2) ✅ YES **Can State 3 reach State 2?** - State 3 → State 2 directly (prob = 1) ✅ YES **So State 2 and State 3 communicate** ↔ **Conclusion:** All states 1, 2, 3 communicate with each other → They form **ONE communicating class** --- ### 🔵 Step 4: Are States Recurrent or Transient? Since all states are in **one communicating class** and the chain is **finite** (only 3 states), all states are **RECURRENT**. ``` Simple Rule: If all states communicate in a finite chain, they are ALL recurrent. Think about it: The ball keeps bouncing between states. It will ALWAYS come back to any state eventually. ``` --- ### 🔵 Step 5: Find the Period of Each State **Period = The MINIMUM number of steps to return to a state** **For State 1:** ``` Can we return to State 1 in 1 step? State 1 → ??? → State 1 in 1 step? State 1 only goes to State 2. State 2 doesn't go back to State 1 in 0 more steps. NO - can't return in 1 step. Can we return to State 1 in 2 steps? State 1 → State 2 → State 1 Probability = 1 × (1/2) = 1/2 > 0 YES! ✅ Can we return in 3 steps? State 1 → State 2 → State 3 → State 2 This ends at State 2, NOT State 1. State 1 → State 2 → State 1 → State 2 This ends at State 2, NOT State 1. So NO path of length 3 returns to State 1. Can we return in 4 steps? State 1 → State 2 → State 3 → State 2 → State 1 Probability = 1 × (1/2) × 1 × (1/2) = 1/4 > 0 YES! ✅ Returns possible at: 2 steps, 4 steps, 6 steps... Period = GCD(2, 4) = 2 ``` **Period of State 1 = 2** --- **For State 2:** ``` Can we return to State 2 in 1 step? State 2 → State 1 → ??? (State 1 goes to State 2) State 2 → State 3 → ??? (State 3 goes to State 2) Both paths go to State 2 in 2 steps, not 1. NO. Can we return to State 2 in 2 steps? Path 1: State 2 → State 1 → State 2 Probability = (1/2) × 1 = 1/2 > 0 ✅ YES! Returns possible at: 2 steps, 4 steps... Period = 2 ``` **Period of State 2 = 2** --- **For State 3:** ``` By similar reasoning (State 3 ↔ State 2 ↔ State 3): State 3 → State 2 → State 3 Probability = 1 × (1/2) = 1/2 > 0 ✅ Returns in 2 steps. Period = 2 ``` **Period of State 3 = 2** --- ### 🔵 Step 6: Final Classification | State | Recurrent/Transient | Period | Ergodic? | |-------|-------------------|--------|----------| | **1** | Recurrent | 2 | ❌ No (periodic) | | **2** | Recurrent | 2 | ❌ No (periodic) | | **3** | Recurrent | 2 | ❌ No (periodic) | ``` All states are: ✅ RECURRENT (they all come back) ❌ NOT ERGODIC (because they are periodic, period = 2) ⚠️ PERIODIC with period 2 This means the chain alternates in a pattern of 2! Example starting from State 1: Step 0: State 1 Step 1: State 2 Step 2: State 1 or 3 Step 3: State 2 Step 4: State 1 or 3 ... Notice State 2 appears at ODD steps ONLY States 1 and 3 appear at EVEN steps ONLY ``` --- ### 🔵 Final Answer: > - **All 3 states are RECURRENT** (the chain always returns) > - **All 3 states have PERIOD = 2** (can only return every 2 steps) > - **No state is ERGODIC** (because all are periodic, not aperiodic) > - **All states form ONE communicating class** --- ## 📊 Quick Summary of All 5 Questions | Q | Topic | Key Skill | Difficulty | |---|-------|-----------|------------| | **Q1** | Basic Transitions | Multiply vector × matrix | ⭐ Easy | | **Q2** | Path Probability | Multiply along a path | ⭐⭐ Easy-Medium | | **Q3** | 2-State Limiting | Solve π P = π | ⭐⭐ Medium | | **Q4** | 3-State Limiting | Solve 3 equations | ⭐⭐⭐ Medium-Hard | | **Q5** | Classification | Draw diagram, find period | ⭐⭐⭐⭐ Hard | --- ## 🎯 Exam Tips ``` 1. ALWAYS check rows of P add to 1.0 2. For PATH probability: P(path) = P(start state) × P₁₂ × P₂₃ × ... 3. For LIMITING probabilities: - Set up πP = π - Add equation: π₁ + π₂ + ... = 1 - Solve the system 4. For PERIOD: - Find shortest path back to same state - Period = GCD of all possible return times 5. ERGODIC = Recurrent + Period 1 (Aperiodic) ```":[5,5]},"outlinks":[],"metadata":{"updated_at":"2026-05-15T21:57:03.344+05:30","edited_seconds":10},"task_lines":[],"tasks":{},"codeblock_ranges":[],"last_embed":{"hash":"1hcqcip","at":1778862431663}},"smart_blocks:Sem 4/DBMS/Unit 5 - Sonnet 4.6.md#Unit 5: Markov Chains - 5 Questions with Full Solutions --- ## 🧠 Before We Start: The Golden Rule of Markov Chains > **\"The future depends ONLY on the present, NOT on the past\"** Think of it like this: If you want to predict tomorrow's weather, you only need to know **today's weather**. It doesn't matter what the weather was last week. --- ## 📖 Basic Concepts You Need to Know ### What is a State? A **state** is simply the **current situation** of a system. - Example: Weather can be in state \"Sunny\" or \"Rainy\" - Example: A student can be in state \"Studying\" or \"Not Studying\" ### What is a Transition Matrix? It's a table that shows **\"If I'm here now, what's the probability I go there next?\"** ``` Example: Weather System Tomorrow Sunny Rainy Today Sunny [ 0.7 0.3 ] Rainy [ 0.4 0.6 ] Reading: If it's Sunny today → 70% chance Sunny tomorrow → 30% chance Rainy tomorrow ``` ### Key Rules: ``` ✅ Every row must ADD UP TO 1.0 ✅ All probabilities must be BETWEEN 0 and 1 ✅ P(n) means the matrix after n steps = P multiplied n times ``` --- --- # ❓ Question 1 (Easy - Basic Transition Probability) ## The Problem: > A college student named Arjun has the following study habits: > - If he **studies** one night, there is a **70% chance he studies** the next night and **30% chance he doesn't** > - If he **doesn't study** one night, there is **60% chance he studies** the next night and **40% chance he doesn't** > > **On Monday night, Arjun studies.** > > Find: > **(i) The Transition Probability Matrix** > **(ii) Probability that Arjun studies on Wednesday night** > **(iii) Probability that Arjun doesn't study on Wednesday night** --- ## ✅ Full Solution: ### 🔵 Step 1: Understand the States We have **2 states**: - **State 1** = Studies (S) - **State 2** = Doesn't Study (D) ``` Think of Arjun switching between two modes every night: Monday → Tuesday → Wednesday → ... S ? ? ``` --- ### 🔵 Step 2: Write the Transition Probability Matrix From the problem: ``` FROM State S (Studies): → Goes to S with probability 0.7 → Goes to D with probability 0.3 FROM State D (Doesn't Study): → Goes to S with probability 0.6 → Goes to D with probability 0.4 ``` $$\\boxed{P = \\begin{bmatrix} 0.7 & 0.3 \\\\ 0.6 & 0.4 \\end{bmatrix}}$$ ``` To S To D From S [ 0.7 0.3 ] From D [ 0.6 0.4 ] ``` **Check: Each row adds to 1:** - Row 1: 0.7 + 0.3 = 1.0 ✅ - Row 2: 0.6 + 0.4 = 1.0 ✅ --- ### 🔵 Step 3: Set Up the Initial State Arjun **studies on Monday** = He starts in **State S** We write this as a **probability vector** (a row showing where we start): $$\\text{Initial vector} = \\begin{bmatrix} 1 & 0 \\end{bmatrix}$$ ``` [1, 0] means: 100% chance he's in State S (Studies) on Monday 0% chance he's in State D ``` --- ### 🔵 Step 4: Find Tuesday's Probabilities (1 step) Multiply the initial vector by P: $$\\text{Tuesday} = \\begin{bmatrix} 1 & 0 \\end{bmatrix} \\times \\begin{bmatrix} 0.7 & 0.3 \\\\ 0.6 & 0.4 \\end{bmatrix}$$ **How to multiply:** ``` Tuesday S probability = (1 × 0.7) + (0 × 0.6) = 0.7 Tuesday D probability = (1 × 0.3) + (0 × 0.4) = 0.3 ``` $$\\text{Tuesday} = \\begin{bmatrix} 0.7 & 0.3 \\end{bmatrix}$$ ``` So on Tuesday: 70% chance Arjun studies 30% chance Arjun doesn't study ``` --- ### 🔵 Step 5: Find Wednesday's Probabilities (2 steps) Multiply Tuesday's vector by P again: $$\\text{Wednesday} = \\begin{bmatrix} 0.7 & 0.3 \\end{bmatrix} \\times \\begin{bmatrix} 0.7 & 0.3 \\\\ 0.6 & 0.4 \\end{bmatrix}$$ **How to multiply:** ``` Wednesday S = (0.7 × 0.7) + (0.3 × 0.6) = 0.49 + 0.18 = 0.67 Wednesday D = (0.7 × 0.3) + (0.3 × 0.4) = 0.21 + 0.12 = 0.33 ``` $$\\text{Wednesday} = \\begin{bmatrix} 0.67 & 0.33 \\end{bmatrix}$$ --- ### 🔵 Final Answers: > **(i)** Transition Matrix: $P = \\begin{bmatrix} 0.7 & 0.3 \\\\ 0.6 & 0.4 \\end{bmatrix}$ > **(ii) Probability Arjun studies on Wednesday = 0.67 (67%)** > **(iii) Probability Arjun doesn't study on Wednesday = 0.33 (33%)** ``` INTUITION CHECK: He starts by studying. Study habit is fairly strong (70%). So it makes sense that 2 days later, there's still a 67% chance he studies. Makes sense! ✅ ``` --- --- # ❓ Question 2 (Medium - Finding Specific Path Probability) ## The Problem: > A Markov chain has **3 states: 1, 2, 3** with the following transition probability matrix: > > $$P = \\begin{bmatrix} 0.1 & 0.5 & 0.4 \\\\ 0.6 & 0.2 & 0.2 \\\\ 0.3 & 0.4 & 0.3 \\end{bmatrix}$$ > > The initial distribution is $P^{(0)} = (0.7, 0.2, 0.1)$ > > Find: > **(i)** $P(X_2 = 3, X_1 = 3, X_0 = 2)$ > **(ii)** $P(X_3 = 2, X_2 = 3, X_1 = 3, X_0 = 2)$ --- ## ✅ Full Solution: ### 🔵 Step 1: Understand What the Question is Asking The notation $X_0, X_1, X_2, X_3$ means the **state at time 0, 1, 2, 3**. ``` Part (i): P(X₂=3, X₁=3, X₀=2) In plain English: \"What is the probability that: - At time 0, the system is in State 2 AND - At time 1, the system is in State 3 AND - At time 2, the system is in State 3\" It's like asking: What's the probability of the PATH: 2 → 3 → 3? ``` --- ### 🔵 Step 2: The Formula for Path Probability $$P(X_2=3, X_1=3, X_0=2) = P(X_0=2) \\times P_{23} \\times P_{33}$$ Where: - $P(X_0 = 2)$ = probability of **starting** in state 2 - $P_{23}$ = probability of going **from state 2 to state 3** - $P_{33}$ = probability of **staying in state 3** ``` Think of it like: Probability of PATH = (Start here) × (Go here) × (Go here) PATH: 2 → 3 → 3 = P(start at 2) × P(2→3) × P(3→3) ``` --- ### 🔵 Step 3: Read Values from the Matrix **Finding P(X₀ = 2):** From initial distribution $(0.7, 0.2, 0.1)$: ``` P(X₀ = 1) = 0.7 P(X₀ = 2) = 0.2 ← This is what we need P(X₀ = 3) = 0.1 ``` **Finding P₂₃ (going from State 2 to State 3):** Look at **Row 2, Column 3** of matrix P: $$P = \\begin{bmatrix} 0.1 & 0.5 & 0.4 \\\\ 0.6 & 0.2 & \\mathbf{0.2} \\\\ 0.3 & 0.4 & 0.3 \\end{bmatrix}$$ ``` Row 2 = [0.6, 0.2, 0.2] ↑ ↑ ↑ P(2→1) P(2→2) P(2→3) So P₂₃ = 0.2 ``` **Finding P₃₃ (staying in State 3):** Look at **Row 3, Column 3**: $$P_{33} = 0.3$$ --- ### 🔵 Step 4: Calculate Part (i) $$P(X_2=3, X_1=3, X_0=2) = P(X_0=2) \\times P_{23} \\times P_{33}$$ $$= 0.2 \\times 0.2 \\times 0.3$$ $$= \\boxed{0.012}$$ ``` Meaning: There's a 1.2% chance of following the path: Start at State 2 → Go to State 3 → Stay at State 3 ``` --- ### 🔵 Step 5: Calculate Part (ii) $$P(X_3=2, X_2=3, X_1=3, X_0=2) = P(X_0=2) \\times P_{23} \\times P_{33} \\times P_{32}$$ **New value needed - P₃₂ (going from State 3 to State 2):** Look at **Row 3, Column 2**: ``` Row 3 = [0.3, 0.4, 0.3] ↑ ↑ ↑ P(3→1) P(3→2) P(3→3) So P₃₂ = 0.4 ``` $$= 0.2 \\times 0.2 \\times 0.3 \\times 0.4$$ $$= 0.012 \\times 0.4$$ $$= \\boxed{0.0048}$$ ``` Meaning: There's a 0.48% chance of following the path: State 2 → State 3 → State 3 → State 2 ``` --- --- # ❓ Question 3 (Medium - Finding Limiting/Steady State Probabilities) ## The Problem: > A weather system can be in two states: > - **State 1:** Sunny > - **State 2:** Rainy > > The transition matrix is: > $$P = \\begin{bmatrix} 0.8 & 0.2 \\\\ 0.5 & 0.5 \\end{bmatrix}$$ > > **Find the long-run probabilities** (i.e., in the long run, what fraction of days will be Sunny and what fraction will be Rainy?) --- ## ✅ Full Solution: ### 🔵 Step 1: Understand What \"Long Run\" Means ``` Imagine the weather system running for 1000 days. After a very long time, the system \"settles\" into a pattern. No matter where you start, you'll find: - Some fixed % of days are Sunny - Some fixed % of days are Rainy These fixed percentages are called LIMITING PROBABILITIES or STEADY STATE PROBABILITIES. We call them: π₁ (for Sunny) and π₂ (for Rainy) ``` --- ### 🔵 Step 2: Set Up the Equations The **magic equation** for limiting probabilities is: $$\\pi P = \\pi$$ In plain English: \"The distribution doesn't change anymore\" Let $\\pi = [\\pi_1, \\pi_2]$ Expanding $\\pi P = \\pi$: $$[\\pi_1, \\pi_2] \\begin{bmatrix} 0.8 & 0.2 \\\\ 0.5 & 0.5 \\end{bmatrix} = [\\pi_1, \\pi_2]$$ --- ### 🔵 Step 3: Multiply the Left Side **For column 1 (Sunny):** ``` (π₁ × 0.8) + (π₂ × 0.5) = π₁ ``` **For column 2 (Rainy):** ``` (π₁ × 0.2) + (π₂ × 0.5) = π₂ ``` --- ### 🔵 Step 4: Solve the Equations **From Equation 1:** $$0.8\\pi_1 + 0.5\\pi_2 = \\pi_1$$ $$0.5\\pi_2 = \\pi_1 - 0.8\\pi_1$$ $$0.5\\pi_2 = 0.2\\pi_1$$ $$\\pi_2 = \\frac{0.2}{0.5}\\pi_1 = 0.4\\pi_1 \\quad \\cdots (A)$$ **We also know probabilities must add to 1:** $$\\pi_1 + \\pi_2 = 1 \\quad \\cdots (B)$$ **Substitute (A) into (B):** $$\\pi_1 + 0.4\\pi_1 = 1$$ $$1.4\\pi_1 = 1$$ $$\\pi_1 = \\frac{1}{1.4} = \\frac{5}{7} \\approx \\boxed{0.714}$$ $$\\pi_2 = 1 - 0.714 = \\boxed{0.286}$$ --- ### 🔵 Step 5: Verify Our Answer Check that $\\pi P = \\pi$: $$[0.714, 0.286] \\begin{bmatrix} 0.8 & 0.2 \\\\ 0.5 & 0.5 \\end{bmatrix}$$ ``` Column 1: (0.714 × 0.8) + (0.286 × 0.5) = 0.5712 + 0.143 = 0.7142 ≈ 0.714 ✅ Column 2: (0.714 × 0.2) + (0.286 × 0.5) = 0.1428 + 0.143 = 0.2858 ≈ 0.286 ✅ ``` --- ### 🔵 Final Answer: > **In the long run:** > - **71.4% of days will be Sunny** > - **28.6% of days will be Rainy** ``` INTUITION CHECK: Sunny days have a STRONG tendency to stay sunny (80%). Rainy days have a weaker tendency to stay rainy (50%). So it makes sense that sunny days dominate in the long run. 71.4% sunny seems very reasonable! ✅ ``` --- --- # ❓ Question 4 (Medium-Hard - 3 State Limiting Probability) ## The Problem: > Three boys A, B, C are throwing a ball to each other. > - **A always throws to B** > - **B always throws to C** > - **C is equally likely to throw to A or B** (50-50 chance) > > Find: > **(i) The Transition Probability Matrix** > **(ii) The limiting probabilities (long run fraction of time each person holds the ball)** --- ## ✅ Full Solution: ### 🔵 Step 1: Understand the System ``` States: A has ball, B has ball, C has ball Let's track where the ball goes: If A has ball → Always throws to B State A → State B (probability = 1) If B has ball → Always throws to C State B → State C (probability = 1) If C has ball → Throws to A or B equally State C → State A (probability = 0.5) State C → State B (probability = 0.5) ``` --- ### 🔵 Step 2: Build the Transition Matrix ``` Ball goes TO: A B C Ball A [ 0 1 0 ] ← A always gives to B from B [ 0 0 1 ] ← B always gives to C C [ 0.5 0.5 0 ] ← C gives to A or B equally ``` $$\\boxed{P = \\begin{bmatrix} 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0.5 & 0.5 & 0 \\end{bmatrix}}$$ **Check rows add to 1:** - Row A: 0 + 1 + 0 = 1 ✅ - Row B: 0 + 0 + 1 = 1 ✅ - Row C: 0.5 + 0.5 + 0 = 1 ✅ --- ### 🔵 Step 3: Set Up Limiting Probability Equations Let $\\pi = [\\pi_A, \\pi_B, \\pi_C]$ Using $\\pi P = \\pi$: $$[\\pi_A, \\pi_B, \\pi_C] \\begin{bmatrix} 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0.5 & 0.5 & 0 \\end{bmatrix} = [\\pi_A, \\pi_B, \\pi_C]$$ --- ### 🔵 Step 4: Write Out the 3 Equations **For πₐ (probability of being at A):** ``` (πₐ × 0) + (π_B × 0) + (π_C × 0.5) = πₐ 0.5π_C = πₐ ... (1) ``` **For π_B (probability of being at B):** ``` (πₐ × 1) + (π_B × 0) + (π_C × 0.5) = π_B πₐ + 0.5π_C = π_B ... (2) ``` **For π_C (probability of being at C):** ``` (πₐ × 0) + (π_B × 1) + (π_C × 0) = π_C π_B = π_C ... (3) ``` **Plus the condition that all probabilities sum to 1:** $$\\pi_A + \\pi_B + \\pi_C = 1 \\quad \\cdots (4)$$ --- ### 🔵 Step 5: Solve the System **From equation (3):** $$\\pi_B = \\pi_C$$ **From equation (1):** $$\\pi_A = 0.5\\pi_C$$ **Substitute into equation (4):** $$0.5\\pi_C + \\pi_C + \\pi_C = 1$$ $$2.5\\pi_C = 1$$ $$\\pi_C = \\frac{1}{2.5} = 0.4$$ **Therefore:** $$\\pi_B = \\pi_C = 0.4$$ $$\\pi_A = 0.5 \\times 0.4 = 0.2$$ --- ### 🔵 Verify with Equation (2): $$\\pi_A + 0.5\\pi_C = \\pi_B$$ $$0.2 + 0.5(0.4) = 0.2 + 0.2 = 0.4 = \\pi_B \\checkmark$$ --- ### 🔵 Final Answer: > **In the long run:** > - **A holds the ball 20% of the time** ($\\pi_A = 0.2$) > - **B holds the ball 40% of the time** ($\\pi_B = 0.4$) > - **C holds the ball 40% of the time** ($\\pi_C = 0.4$) ``` INTUITION CHECK: - A only RECEIVES the ball from C (50% of C's throws) - B RECEIVES from both A (100%) and C (50%) - C RECEIVES from B (100%) B and C both receive from multiple sources, so they hold the ball more often than A. Makes total sense! ✅ ``` --- --- # ❓ Question 5 (Hard - Classification of States) ## The Problem: > A Markov chain has the following transition probability matrix: > > $$P = \\begin{bmatrix} 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1/2 & 0 & 1/2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\end{bmatrix}$$ > > **Classify all the states of this Markov chain.** > (Are they Transient or Recurrent? Periodic or Aperiodic? Ergodic?) --- ## ✅ Full Solution: ### 🔵 Step 1: Understand the Classification Terms ``` TRANSIENT STATE: → The system MIGHT never come back here → Like a bus stop where buses might skip you RECURRENT STATE: → The system WILL definitely come back here eventually → Like your home - you always return PERIODIC STATE (period d): → You can only return to this state every d steps → Example: Period 2 means you return after 2, 4, 6... steps only APERIODIC STATE: → Period = 1 → You can return at any time (2 steps, 3 steps, etc.) ERGODIC STATE: → Recurrent + Aperiodic → Best kind of state for analysis ``` --- ### 🔵 Step 2: Draw the Transition Diagram First, let's see which states can reach which: $$P = \\begin{bmatrix} 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1/2 & 0 & 1/2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\end{bmatrix}$$ ``` Reading the matrix: State 1: Goes to State 2 (prob=1), nowhere else State 2: Goes to State 1 (prob=1/2) or State 3 (prob=1/2) State 3: Goes to State 2 (prob=1), nowhere else Drawing the diagram: (1/2) (1/2) 1 ←─────── 2 ──────────→ 3 │ ↑ │ └──────────→│ │ (1) │←────────────┘ (1) (1) ``` More clearly: ``` 1 ──(prob 1)──→ 2 ──(prob 1/2)──→ 3 ↑ │ │ │ │ (prob 1/2) │ (prob 1) └─────────────────────┘ │ ↑ │ └────────────────────────┘ ``` --- ### 🔵 Step 3: Check if States Communicate Two states **communicate** if you can get from one to the other AND back. **Can State 1 reach State 2?** - State 1 → State 2 directly (prob = 1) ✅ YES **Can State 2 reach State 1?** - State 2 → State 1 directly (prob = 1/2) ✅ YES **So State 1 and State 2 communicate** ↔ **Can State 2 reach State 3?** - State 2 → State 3 directly (prob = 1/2) ✅ YES **Can State 3 reach State 2?** - State 3 → State 2 directly (prob = 1) ✅ YES **So State 2 and State 3 communicate** ↔ **Conclusion:** All states 1, 2, 3 communicate with each other → They form **ONE communicating class** --- ### 🔵 Step 4: Are States Recurrent or Transient? Since all states are in **one communicating class** and the chain is **finite** (only 3 states), all states are **RECURRENT**. ``` Simple Rule: If all states communicate in a finite chain, they are ALL recurrent. Think about it: The ball keeps bouncing between states. It will ALWAYS come back to any state eventually. ``` --- ### 🔵 Step 5: Find the Period of Each State **Period = The MINIMUM number of steps to return to a state** **For State 1:** ``` Can we return to State 1 in 1 step? State 1 → ??? → State 1 in 1 step? State 1 only goes to State 2. State 2 doesn't go back to State 1 in 0 more steps. NO - can't return in 1 step. Can we return to State 1 in 2 steps? State 1 → State 2 → State 1 Probability = 1 × (1/2) = 1/2 > 0 YES! ✅ Can we return in 3 steps? State 1 → State 2 → State 3 → State 2 This ends at State 2, NOT State 1. State 1 → State 2 → State 1 → State 2 This ends at State 2, NOT State 1. So NO path of length 3 returns to State 1. Can we return in 4 steps? State 1 → State 2 → State 3 → State 2 → State 1 Probability = 1 × (1/2) × 1 × (1/2) = 1/4 > 0 YES! ✅ Returns possible at: 2 steps, 4 steps, 6 steps... Period = GCD(2, 4) = 2 ``` **Period of State 1 = 2** --- **For State 2:** ``` Can we return to State 2 in 1 step? State 2 → State 1 → ??? (State 1 goes to State 2) State 2 → State 3 → ??? (State 3 goes to State 2) Both paths go to State 2 in 2 steps, not 1. NO. Can we return to State 2 in 2 steps? Path 1: State 2 → State 1 → State 2 Probability = (1/2) × 1 = 1/2 > 0 ✅ YES! Returns possible at: 2 steps, 4 steps... Period = 2 ``` **Period of State 2 = 2** --- **For State 3:** ``` By similar reasoning (State 3 ↔ State 2 ↔ State 3): State 3 → State 2 → State 3 Probability = 1 × (1/2) = 1/2 > 0 ✅ Returns in 2 steps. Period = 2 ``` **Period of State 3 = 2** --- ### 🔵 Step 6: Final Classification | State | Recurrent/Transient | Period | Ergodic? | |-------|-------------------|--------|----------| | **1** | Recurrent | 2 | ❌ No (periodic) | | **2** | Recurrent | 2 | ❌ No (periodic) | | **3** | Recurrent | 2 | ❌ No (periodic) | ``` All states are: ✅ RECURRENT (they all come back) ❌ NOT ERGODIC (because they are periodic, period = 2) ⚠️ PERIODIC with period 2 This means the chain alternates in a pattern of 2! Example starting from State 1: Step 0: State 1 Step 1: State 2 Step 2: State 1 or 3 Step 3: State 2 Step 4: State 1 or 3 ... Notice State 2 appears at ODD steps ONLY States 1 and 3 appear at EVEN steps ONLY ``` --- ### 🔵 Final Answer: > - **All 3 states are RECURRENT** (the chain always returns) > - **All 3 states have PERIOD = 2** (can only return every 2 steps) > - **No state is ERGODIC** (because all are periodic, not aperiodic) > - **All states form ONE communicating class** --- ## 📊 Quick Summary of All 5 Questions | Q | Topic | Key Skill | Difficulty | |---|-------|-----------|------------| | **Q1** | Basic Transitions | Multiply vector × matrix | ⭐ Easy | | **Q2** | Path Probability | Multiply along a path | ⭐⭐ Easy-Medium | | **Q3** | 2-State Limiting | Solve π P = π | ⭐⭐ Medium | | **Q4** | 3-State Limiting | Solve 3 equations | ⭐⭐⭐ Medium-Hard | | **Q5** | Classification | Draw diagram, find period | ⭐⭐⭐⭐ Hard | --- ## 🎯 Exam Tips ``` 1. ALWAYS check rows of P add to 1.0 2. For PATH probability: P(path) = P(start state) × P₁₂ × P₂₃ × ... 3. For LIMITING probabilities: - Set up πP = π - Add equation: π₁ + π₂ + ... = 1 - Solve the system 4. For PERIOD: - Find shortest path back to same state - Period = GCD of all possible return times 5. ERGODIC = Recurrent + Period 1 (Aperiodic) ```": {"path":null,"embeddings":{"TaylorAI/bge-micro-v2":{"vec":[-0.11088292,-0.04100923,0.01688456,0.01781268,0.03552063,0.05065673,0.03609453,0.00986471,0.03422002,0.0802726,-0.01401439,0.00359935,-0.00014453,0.06938443,-0.01442533,0.02414172,-0.02567543,0.01993169,-0.01349772,0.02594217,0.06709067,-0.0094279,-0.05180469,-0.05559821,0.06551092,0.0680932,0.03130857,-0.00145175,-0.03041026,-0.21355467,-0.02129871,-0.03841847,-0.02788749,-0.04084256,-0.00793964,-0.04554246,0.01841703,0.02129611,-0.03201124,0.02143672,-0.03495738,0.06178455,-0.01008204,-0.02846723,-0.04333687,-0.04385179,-0.00763638,-0.00968742,-0.06066856,-0.03367196,-0.0370186,0.00475261,-0.03458237,0.02986905,0.03850352,0.03400508,0.07170346,0.04113871,-0.02021318,0.0066538,-0.00049576,0.01656254,-0.22476137,0.06836064,0.06591146,0.01823876,-0.02500213,0.03265381,0.05231807,0.1038857,-0.07248118,0.02718067,-0.00811161,0.09515457,-0.00519233,-0.00913072,-0.00442899,-0.03995369,0.00771775,-0.0106714,-0.01554252,0.0180736,-0.00758254,-0.05471314,-0.05060373,0.04868065,0.00545438,-0.01508438,0.04770639,-0.0005804,0.00214444,-0.00042633,-0.02978718,0.05884053,0.00607373,0.04253614,0.08869983,0.01814467,0.00750618,0.10242073,-0.03006174,0.00389395,0.04352857,0.03379733,0.03725798,-0.01247298,-0.00629485,-0.08141735,-0.02296945,-0.04199183,0.01282984,-0.02538704,0.0466